WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.274
Indexes are physical structures that help PostgreSQL

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.550
find rows more efficiently Primary keys and

00:00:07.576 --> 00:00:11.352
UNIQUE constraints automatically receive supporting
unique indexes

00:00:13.095 --> 00:00:15.305
Foreign-key columns on the child side are

00:00:15.331 --> 00:00:19.624
different PostgreSQL does not automatically create
every

00:00:19.637 --> 00:00:23.637
child-side index for you Whether an index

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.018
is useful depends on actual query patterns

00:00:26.176 --> 00:00:31.110
joins updates deletes and data volume Run

00:00:31.137 --> 00:00:33.913
the provided example and inspect the indexes

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.638
At this stage you do not need

00:00:37.651 --> 00:00:40.917
to design a full indexing strategy The

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.641
important idea is that logical relationships and

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.351
physical performance structures are related but not

00:00:46.378 --> 00:00:51.330
identical First design correct keys and constraints

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.398
Then add indexes based on real access

00:00:54.411 --> 00:00:56.411
patterns and measured performance needs
